{"title":"Community Health Officer Roadmap","role":"Community Health Officer","summary":"A Community Health Officer (CHO) works at the community and primary-care level, supporting preventive healthcare, health promotion, screening, disease surveillance, referrals, health education and community-based health programs.","facts":{"Current degree":"Allied Health Sciences (B.Sc / BPT / BOT / BMLT etc.)","Course":"Public Health (BPH / B.Sc Public Health)"},"about":[],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Career Overview"},{"type":"p","text":"A Community Health Officer (CHO) works at the community and primary-care level, supporting preventive healthcare, health promotion, screening, disease surveillance, referrals, health education and community-based health programs."},{"type":"p","text":"The role can involve working with Primary Health Centres, Health & Wellness Centres, community-health programs, NGOs, public-health projects and other primary-care organizations."},{"type":"p","text":"For a Public Health graduate, the career is particularly suitable for students interested in community medicine, epidemiology, preventive healthcare, health programs and population health."},{"type":"note","label":"Important","text":"CHO eligibility and recruitment requirements vary by state, program and recruiting authority. In India, some CHO positions are specifically restricted to particular clinical qualifications such as nursing/AYUSH backgrounds or require additional bridge/certificate training. A BPH/B.Sc Public Health degree should therefore be checked against the exact recruitment notification before treating CHO as a guaranteed government-job pathway."}]},{"heading":"Career Horizon","kind":"horizons","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Stage","Focus","Key Capabilities"],"rows":[[{"text":"Year 1"},{"text":"Public-health foundation"},{"text":"Epidemiology, health systems, communication"}],[{"text":"Year 2"},{"text":